  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 328 PM CDT Tue Sep 8 2026\n\nActive weather returns on Friday as a cold front approaches the \narea. Increased moisture ahead of the front will provide a favorable \nenvironment for thunderstorm development by Friday afternoon. Ample \ninstability will exist across much of the region into Friday evening \nallowing for severe thunderstorms to develop. Initially, \nthunderstorms will be discrete in nature with a very large hail \npotential. As the evening progresses, thunderstorms will congeal \ninto a line becoming more of a damaging wind threat. Current SPC \noutlooks for day 4 highlight this potential for severe thunderstorms \nacross the area as well, which seems to be in good agreement with \ncurrent thinking at this time. \n\nUnsettled weather will continue through the middle of next week as a \nseries of disturbances pivot across the region. While the severe \nrisk remains uncertain beyond Friday night/Saturday morning, some \nstronger to briefly severe storms will be entirely possible with \nthunderstorms mainly confined to the late afternoon and early \nevening hours, weakening overnight as more stable air moves into the \narea after sunset. Will continue to monitor the severe potential \nheading into the weekend and into next week. \n\nTemperatures will remain above normal in the low to mid 90s on \nFriday, however with the cold front passage on Friday evening, highs \nwill drop significantly for the weekend and into next week. The \ncooler airmass, combined with continued clouds and precipitation \nwill keep highs from rising out of the 70s through next Tuesday. \nOvernight lows will also drop drastically through early next week. \nExpect temperatures to range from the low 40s in the northwest to \nthe low 50s into south central Nebraska.",
